      "persona": "Whitehat Hacker",
      "taskMessage": "<b>Dr. Mira Sen:</b> Fragmented packets are in this module because defenders need to know that some inspection paths reassemble traffic badly. We look at that weakness so we can describe it inside a controlled test, never to slip a scan past somebody's monitoring. The distinction is the whole reason this stays within an authorized scope.",
      "taskInstructions": "Run <b>hping3 -S -f -p 443 10.42.18.73</b> and use the logged defensive response as context.",
      "taskQuestion": "Why is fragmentation covered here?",
      "taskCompleteText": "<b>Dr. Mira Sen:</b> Correct. Fragmentation is covered to understand defensive behavior within controlled validation.",
      "answers": [
        "To evade monitoring during an unauthorized scan of the range.",
        "To prove that IPv6 is disabled on the target host.",
        "To replace ordinary service detection entirely.",
        "To understand firewall and IDS behavior in a controlled validation."
      ],
      "correctAnswer": 3
    },
    {
      "persona": "Blackhat Hacker",
      "taskMessage": "<b>Kade Mercer:</b> Fragmentation is a standing reminder that controls have seams, and seams are where the interesting things live. That said, this exercise has an edge to it and we are staying on this side of the edge. Knowing a blind spot exists is a finding; walking through it on this engagement would be something else entirely.",
